<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="JirAI: Timeline, comments and breakdown" id="Jira-Timeline-and-Description-Editor">

    <primary-label ref="jirai"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <link-summary>Follow an issue's activity, post and edit comments, edit the description, and filter the issue's child work items.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Read the activity feed, post and edit comments, edit the description in Markdown and browse child work items.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Use the JirAI issue timeline in JetBrains IDEs: Jira Cloud comments and comment editing, history, Markdown description editing, and the Breakdown tree of child work items with filters.</web-summary>

    <p>Each issue you open gets an editor tab named after its key, for example <control>DEMO-123 Timeline</control>.</p>
    <p>To open it, select an issue in the <control>JirAI</control> tool window and press <shortcut>Enter</shortcut>.</p>
    <p>The tab has two inner tabs: <control>Timeline</control> for activity and discussion, and <control>Breakdown</control> for child work items.</p>
    <p>For a work item whose type cannot have child work items, such as a sub-task, only the <control>Timeline</control> tab is shown.</p>

    <chapter title="Read the activity feed" id="timeline">
        <p>The <control>Timeline</control> tab shows the description, comments, status changes and field changes in one feed.</p>
        <p>To show only one kind of entry, click the filter icon and choose <control>All</control>, <control>Comments</control>, <control>Status changes</control> or <control>Field changes</control>.</p>
        <p>To load the latest activity, right-click the feed and choose <control>Refresh</control>, or press the IDE's <control>Refresh</control> shortcut.</p>
        <p>The same right-click menu also has these items:</p>
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Open in Jira">Opens the issue on your Jira site.</def>
            <def title="Copy Issue URL">Copies the issue's Jira link to the clipboard.</def>
            <def title="Copy Issue Key">Copies the issue key, for example <code>DEMO-123</code>, to the clipboard.</def>
        </deflist>
        <img src="jirai_timeline.png" alt="Issue timeline with the description and the sprint and assignee changes" width="642" border-effect="rounded"/>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Comment on an issue" id="comments">
        <procedure title="Post a comment" id="post-comment-proc">
            <step><p>Click the <control>Add a comment…</control> field at the bottom of the timeline.</p></step>
            <step><p>Type your comment.</p></step>
            <step><p>Optional: type <code>@</code> and choose a person to mention them.</p></step>
            <step><p>Optional: click the paperclip to attach files.</p></step>
            <step><p>Click <control>Post</control>, or press <shortcut>Ctrl+Enter</shortcut> (macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ⏎</shortcut>).</p></step>
        </procedure>
        <p>Press <shortcut>Enter</shortcut> to start a new line in the comment.</p>
        <p>Files you attach to a comment are added to the issue's attachments and shown in the comment.</p>
        <p>To delete one of your comments, click the delete icon on it and confirm.</p>
        <p>Jira has no undo for deleted comments, and you can delete only the comments that Jira permissions allow.</p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Edit a comment" id="edit-comment">
        <secondary-label ref="jirai_version"/>
        <p>You can edit a comment in place when Jira lets you edit it.</p>
        <procedure title="Edit a comment" id="edit-comment-proc">
            <step><p>Hover over the comment and click the pencil icon (<control>Edit comment</control>).</p></step>
            <step>
                <p>Edit the text and its formatting in Markdown.</p>
                <!-- planned-image src="jirai_comment_edit.png" alt="Comment being edited in place with a locked mention and the Cancel and Save buttons" -->
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Click <control>Save</control>, or press <shortcut>Ctrl+Enter</shortcut> (macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ⏎</shortcut>).</p>
                <p>To discard your changes, click <control>Cancel</control> or press <shortcut>Escape</shortcut>.</p>
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p><control>Cancel</control> and the highlighted <control>Save</control> button sit at the bottom right of the editor.</p>
        <p>You can edit one comment at a time in each issue tab.</p>

        <chapter title="Locked pieces" id="comment-locked-pieces">
            <p>Mentions, images, tables, panels and anything else the editor cannot show appear as locked pieces, for example <control>🔒 Table (3×2)</control>.</p>
            <list>
                <li><p>JirAI saves each locked piece exactly as it was.</p></li>
                <li><p>You can move a locked piece or delete it, but you cannot change it.</p></li>
                <li><p>A locked block must stay on a line of its own, outside lists, headings and code.</p></li>
            </list>
            <p>If an edit breaks a locked piece, the editor says what is wrong and does not save.</p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Comments you can edit only in Jira" id="comment-readonly">
            <p>A comment restricted to a role or group, and a service-desk comment, can be edited only in Jira.</p>
            <p>For such a comment, the editor explains why and offers <control>Open in browser</control>.</p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="When someone else changed the comment" id="comment-conflict">
            <p>If the comment changed in Jira while you were editing, the editor reads <control>This comment was changed in Jira while you were editing.</control></p>
            <p>Click <control>Load theirs</control> to replace your text with the version from Jira.</p>
            <p>If you close the issue tab during an edit, JirAI keeps your text.</p>
            <p>The comment then reads <control>You have an unsaved edit of this comment.</control> with <control>Resume</control> and <control>Discard</control>.</p>
        </chapter>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Edit the description" id="description-editor">
        <p>You edit the description in place, on the description card at the top of the timeline.</p>
        <p>JirAI converts Jira's description format to Markdown for editing and converts it back when you save.</p>
        <procedure title="Edit the description" id="edit-description-proc">
            <step><p>On the description card, click the pencil icon (<control>Edit description</control>).</p></step>
            <step>
                <p>Edit the Markdown text.</p>
                <img src="jirai_description_edit.png" alt="Description being edited in place with the Save and Cancel buttons" width="642" border-effect="rounded"/>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Click <control>Save</control>, or press <shortcut>Ctrl+Enter</shortcut> (macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ⏎</shortcut>).</p>
                <p>To discard your changes, click <control>Cancel</control> or press <shortcut>Escape</shortcut>.</p>
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p><control>Cancel</control> and the highlighted <control>Save</control> button sit at the bottom right of the card.</p>
        <p>If you close the issue tab while you edit, JirAI keeps your unsaved description.</p>
        <p>The next time you open the issue, the card offers <control>Resume</control> and <control>Discard</control>.</p>
        <p>After you save, the timeline shows an <control>Updated the description</control> entry.</p>
        <p>Click <control>Show changes</control> on it to see what changed as a diff.</p>
        <img src="jirai_description_diff.png" alt="Timeline entry showing the description change as a diff" width="642" border-effect="rounded"/>

        <chapter title="When someone else changed the description" id="description-conflict">
            <p>Before saving, JirAI checks whether the description changed in Jira since you started editing.</p>
            <p>If it did, the card reads <control>The description was changed in Jira while you were editing.</control></p>
            <list>
                <li><p>Click <control>Load theirs</control> to replace your text with the version from Jira.</p></li>
                <li><p>If the new version can no longer be edited in the IDE, click <control>Copy my text and close</control> to keep your text on the clipboard.</p></li>
            </list>
            <p>The check catches a change made between loading and saving; it is not a lock.</p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Descriptions you can edit only in Jira" id="description-readonly">
            <p>Some Jira content has no Markdown equivalent, and saving it from the IDE would lose it.</p>
            <p>A description that contains such content is shown but cannot be edited in the IDE.</p>
            <p>The card explains why, for example <control>This description contains a table, so it can only be edited in Jira.</control>, and offers <control>Open in browser</control>.</p>
            <p>This applies, among others, to descriptions with:</p>
            <list columns="2">
                <li>tables and panels</li>
                <li>images and attachments</li>
                <li>smart links</li>
                <li>@mentions and emoji</li>
                <li>checklists and decision lists</li>
                <li>quotes and dividers</li>
                <li>expandable sections</li>
                <li>status lozenges and dates</li>
                <li>column layouts and synced blocks</li>
                <li>app extensions</li>
                <li>underline, text color, subscript and superscript</li>
                <li>inline comments</li>
            </list>
            <!-- planned-image src="jirai_description_readonly.png" alt="Description that contains a table, shown as read-only with Open in browser" -->
        </chapter>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The Breakdown tab" id="breakdown-tab">
        <p>The <control>Breakdown</control> tab lists the issue's child work items, sub-tasks included.</p>
        <p>Its title shows the count, for example <control>Breakdown (6)</control>.</p>
        <list>
            <li><p>More children load as you scroll.</p></li>
            <li><p>Click a child to open it.</p></li>
            <li><p>Right-click a child for the same menu as in the issue list, including <control>Copy Issue URL</control> and <control>Create Branch…</control>. See <a href="Work-with-Jira-Issues.topic" anchor="row-actions">Issue actions</a>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Click <control>Parent: KEY ↗</control> at the top to open the parent of the current issue.</p></li>
            <li><p>Click <control>Add child</control> to create a child work item with this issue as its parent. See <a href="Jira-Create-Issues.topic" anchor="add-child">Create a child work item</a>.</p></li>
        </list>
        <!-- planned-image src="jirai_breakdown_tab.png" alt="Breakdown tab of an epic listing its child work items with the parent link and Add child" -->
        <p>An issue without children shows <control>No child work items.</control></p>

        <chapter title="Show the whole hierarchy" id="breakdown-hierarchy">
            <secondary-label ref="jirai_version"/>
            <p><control>Show Hierarchy</control> on the filter bar is on by default.</p>
            <p>It shows the whole tree under the issue, with each child indented under its parent.</p>
            <list>
                <li><p>Click the chevron on a row to fold or unfold its children.</p></li>
                <li><p>Click <control>Expand All</control> or <control>Collapse All</control> to unfold or fold every row.</p></li>
                <li><p>Turn <control>Show Hierarchy</control> off to list only the direct children.</p></li>
            </list>
            <p>While a filter is active, the list is flat, and the tooltip of <control>Show Hierarchy</control> says so.</p>
            <p>The tree is not available for a work item with more than 1,000 descendants; the list then shows the direct children.</p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Filter and sort the children" id="breakdown-filters">
            <secondary-label ref="jirai_version"/>
            <p>The <control>Breakdown</control> tab has the filter bar of the <control>JirAI</control> tool window, without the <control>Project</control> and <control>Epic</control> chips.</p>
            <list>
                <li><p>Type in <control>Search issues</control> to match the children's text.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use the <control>Status</control>, <control>Type</control>, <control>Priority</control>, <control>Assignee</control> and <control>Reporter</control> chips. The <control>Status</control> popup also offers <control>Unresolved</control>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Click <control>Sort By</control> to sort by <control>Updated</control>, <control>Created</control> or <control>Priority</control>. The list starts sorted by creation date, oldest first.</p></li>
            </list>
            <p>When nothing matches, the tab shows a <control>Clear filters</control> link.</p>
            <p>Filters and sort apply while the issue tab is open; they are not saved.</p>
            <p>See <a href="Work-with-Jira-Issues.topic" anchor="filters">Filter the list</a> for how the chips work.</p>
        </chapter>
    </chapter>

